---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Experiment 12.1: A Simulation of Plastic Rock | C00lnerd">
    <div class="experiment-container">
        <h1>Experiment 12.1: A Simulation of Plastic Rock</h1>
        
        <div class="student-info">
            <h2>Student Information</h2>
            <form id="studentInfoForm">
                <div class="form-group">
                    <label for="studentName">Student Name:</label>
                    <input type="text" id="studentName" name="studentName" required>
                </div>
                <div class="form-group">
                    <label for="studentEmail">Student Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="studentEmail" name="studentEmail" required>
                </div>
                <div class="form-group">
                    <label for="instructorEmail">Instructor Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="instructorEmail" name="instructorEmail" value="chuck@c00lnerd.com" readonly>
                </div>
            </form>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Purpose</h2>
            <p>To explore the properties of plastic rock by producing a mixture that simulates such behavior.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Background Information</h2>
            <p>Most theories that describe Earth's mantle have plastic rock moving about in huge currents in the asthenosphere. The asthenosphere is a layer of the Earth's mantle that behaves like warm taffy candy, flowing around in the mantle. It's important to note that these rocks in the mantle are not liquid, just as taffy is not liquid. When subjected to an abrupt force, the flowing rocks can harden into a firm solid. After the force passes, the rock returns to its flowing state.</p>
            
            <p>Below the asthenosphere is the mesosphere. The rocks of the mesosphere are stiffer than those of the asthenosphere. Despite being stiffer, these rocks can still flow only much more slowly. Scientists call these rocks "stiff" plastic rock.</p>
            
            <p>This experiment will help you understand how plastic rock behaves by creating a cornstarch and water mixture that exhibits similar properties.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Materials</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>A shallow pan (a pie pan, for example)</li>
                <li>Cornstarch</li>
                <li>Measuring cups</li>
                <li>Water</li>
                <li>Spoon for stirring</li>
                <li>Eye protection such as goggles or safety glasses</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Safety</h2>
            <div class="safety-warning">
                <p><strong>IMPORTANT:</strong> Always wear eye protection during this experiment. While cornstarch is not particularly dangerous, it's good practice to protect your eyes during any science experiment.</p>
                <p>Be careful with cleanup - the mixture can clog drains if not properly diluted. Follow the cleanup instructions carefully.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Procedure</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>Put one cup of cornstarch into the pan (237mL or 8oz).</li>
                <li>Add 2/3 cup of water (158mL or 5.3oz).</li>
                <li>Stir the water and cornstarch together. You should find it very hard to stir at first, but as you continue to stir, you should get to the point where you have an evenly mixed, white liquid in the pan.</li>
                <li>Add an additional 1/4 cup of cornstarch to the mixture.</li>
                <li>Stir the newly added cornstarch into the mixture. Now you should start seeing an interesting behavior: As you stir, the mixture should solidify, making it hard to stir. However, when you stop stirring and let the mixture sit, it should liquefy again. If you don't see that behavior, your mixture is not correct. If the mixture stays liquid, you have too much water. To fix this, add cornstarch a little at a time until you get the desired behavior. If the mixture never turns to liquid, you have too much cornstarch. Add water a little at a time until you get the desired behavior.</li>
                <li>Explore this interesting behavior for a while. Describe your observations in the data table.</li>
                <li>Pick up some of the mixture with your hand. As you pick it up, it should become solid.</li>
                <li>Squeeze the solid into a ball, then relax your hand and watch what happens to the ball.</li>
                <li>Repeat this a few times. Describe your observations in the data table.</li>
                <li>To clean up everything, you will need to use hot water from the tap. As you add hot water to the pan, the mixture will become a liquid. Continue to add water until it is very fluid and you can safely pour it down the drain. Make sure to leave the hot water running for several minutes after the mixture has been cleaned up, as you need to flush it down the drain. Failure to do so could clog your drain.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Data Collection</h2>
            <table class="data-table">
                <tr>
                    <th>Observation Point</th>
                    <th>Description of Behavior</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Initial mixing behavior</td>
                    <td><textarea id="observation1" rows="3"></textarea></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Behavior when stirring</td>
                    <td><textarea id="observation2" rows="3"></textarea></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Behavior when still</td>
                    <td><textarea id="observation3" rows="3"></textarea></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Behavior when picking up</td>
                    <td><textarea id="observation4" rows="3"></textarea></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Behavior when squeezing into a ball</td>
                    <td><textarea id="observation5" rows="3"></textarea></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Behavior when relaxing hand</td>
                    <td><textarea id="observation6" rows="3"></textarea></td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Analysis Questions</h2>
            <div class="question">
                <p>1. How does the cornstarch mixture's behavior relate to plastic rock in the Earth's mantle?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis1" rows="4"></textarea>
            </div>
            <div class="question">
                <p>2. What happens to the mixture when force is applied? How is this similar to what happens in the asthenosphere?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis2" rows="4"></textarea>
            </div>
            <div class="question">
                <p>3. How does the behavior of the mixture help explain the difference between the asthenosphere and mesosphere?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis3" rows="4"></textarea>
            </div>
            <div class="question">
                <p>4. Why do you think scientists use models like this to understand Earth's interior?</p>
                <textarea id="analysis4" rows="4"></textarea>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Conclusion</h2>
            <p>Did this experiment help you understand the properties of plastic rock? Explain your observations and make connections to the text.</p>
            <textarea id="conclusion" rows="6"></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="experiment-section">
            <h2>Extension Activities</h2>
            <p>Try these additional activities to further explore the properties of non-Newtonian fluids like your cornstarch mixture:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Try placing a small speaker playing bass-heavy music under a thin layer of the mixture. Observe how the vibrations affect the fluid.</li>
                <li>Try different ratios of cornstarch to water and record how the behavior changes.</li>
                <li>Research other examples of non-Newtonian fluids and how they are used in real-world applications.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="submit-section">
            <button id="submitButton" class="submit-button">Submit Results</button>
            <div id="submissionStatus"></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>

<style>
    .experiment-container {
        max-width: 800px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 20px;
    }
    
    h1 {
        color: #2c3e50;
        text-align: center;
        margin-bottom: 30px;
    }
    
    h2 {
        color: #3498db;
        margin-top: 30px;
        border-bottom: 2px solid #3498db;
        padding-bottom: 5px;
    }
    
    .experiment-section {
        margin-bottom: 30px;
    }
    
    .form-group {
        margin-bottom: 15px;
    }
    
    label {
        display: block;
        margin-bottom: 5px;
        font-weight: bold;
    }
    
    input[type="text"],
    input[type="email"],
    textarea {
        width: 100%;
        padding: 8px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        border-radius: 4px;
    }
    
    .safety-warning {
        background-color: #ffeeee;
        border-left: 4px solid #ff5555;
        padding: 15px;
        margin: 15px 0;
    }
    
    ol, ul {
        margin-left: 20px;
        line-height: 1.6;
    }
    
    .data-table {
        width: 100%;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        margin: 20px 0;
    }
    
    .data-table th, .data-table td {
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        padding: 10px;
    }
    
    .data-table th {
        background-color: #f2f2f2;
        text-align: left;
    }
    
    .question {
        margin-bottom: 20px;
    }
    
    .submit-button {
        background-color: #3498db;
        color: white;
        border: none;
        padding: 10px 20px;
        font-size: 16px;
        border-radius: 4px;
        cursor: pointer;
        transition: background-color 0.3s;
    }
    
    .submit-button:hover {
        background-color: #2980b9;
    }
    
    #submissionStatus {
        margin-top: 15px;
        font-weight: bold;
    }
    
    .success {
        color: green;
    }
    
    .error {
        color: red;
    }
</style>

<script>
    // Store student email in local storage
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
        // Load saved email if available
        const savedEmail = localStorage.getItem('studentEmail');
        if (savedEmail) {
            document.getElementById('studentEmail').value = savedEmail;
        }
        
        // Save email when entered
        document.getElementById('studentEmail').addEventListener('change', function() {
            localStorage.setItem('studentEmail', this.value);
        });
        
        // Handle form submission
        document.getElementById('submitButton').addEventListener('click', function() {
            submitResults();
        });
    });
    
    function submitResults() {
        // Get form values
        const studentName = document.getElementById('studentName').value;
        const studentEmail = document.getElementById('studentEmail').value;
        const instructorEmail = document.getElementById('instructorEmail').value;
        
        // Validate form
        if (!studentName || !studentEmail) {
            showStatus('Please fill in all required fields.', 'error');
            return;
        }
        
        // Collect observations
        const observations = {
            initial: document.getElementById('observation1').value,
            stirring: document.getElementById('observation2').value,
            still: document.getElementById('observation3').value,
            pickup: document.getElementById('observation4').value,
            squeezing: document.getElementById('observation5').value,
            relaxing: document.getElementById('observation6').value
        };
        
        // Collect analysis answers
        const analysis = {
            q1: document.getElementById('analysis1').value,
            q2: document.getElementById('analysis2').value,
            q3: document.getElementById('analysis3').value,
            q4: document.getElementById('analysis4').value
        };
        
        const conclusion = document.getElementById('conclusion').value;
        
        // Extract student name from email if not provided
        const extractedName = studentName || (studentEmail ? studentEmail.split('@')[0] : 'Student');
        
        // Check for missing elements
        const missingElements = [];
        if (!observations.initial) missingElements.push('Initial Observation');
        if (!observations.stirring) missingElements.push('Stirring Observation');
        if (!observations.still) missingElements.push('Still Observation');
        if (!observations.pickup) missingElements.push('Pickup Observation');
        if (!observations.squeezing) missingElements.push('Squeezing Observation');
        if (!observations.relaxing) missingElements.push('Relaxing Observation');
        if (!analysis.q1) missingElements.push('Analysis Question 1');
        if (!analysis.q2) missingElements.push('Analysis Question 2');
        if (!analysis.q3) missingElements.push('Analysis Question 3');
        if (!analysis.q4) missingElements.push('Analysis Question 4');
        if (!conclusion) missingElements.push('Conclusion');
        
        const missingElementsText = missingElements.length > 0 ? 
            `❌ Missing: ${missingElements.join(', ')}` : 
            '✅ All sections completed';

        // Format comprehensive experiment data
        const conclusionText = `
EXPERIMENT 12.1: A SIMULATION OF PLASTIC ROCK RESULTS
===================================================

Student: ${extractedName}
Email: ${studentEmail}

OBSERVATIONS:
• Initial Observation: ${observations.initial || 'Not provided'}
• During Stirring: ${observations.stirring || 'Not provided'}
• When Still: ${observations.still || 'Not provided'}
• When Picked Up: ${observations.pickup || 'Not provided'}
• When Squeezing: ${observations.squeezing || 'Not provided'}
• When Relaxing: ${observations.relaxing || 'Not provided'}

ANALYSIS QUESTIONS:
1. How did the mixture behave when you first stirred it?
${analysis.q1 || 'Not answered'}

2. What happened when you let the mixture sit still?
${analysis.q2 || 'Not answered'}

3. How did the mixture respond when you picked it up and squeezed it?
${analysis.q3 || 'Not answered'}

4. How does this experiment relate to the formation of plastic rock?
${analysis.q4 || 'Not answered'}

CONCLUSION:
${conclusion || 'Not provided'}

DATA COMPLETENESS: ${missingElementsText}
        `.trim();

        const templateParams = {
            student_name: extractedName,
            student_email: studentEmail,
            experiment_title: 'Experiment 12.1: A Simulation of Plastic Rock',
            hypothesis: 'Simulating plastic rock formation through layered material compression',
            missing_elements: missingElementsText,
            conclusion: conclusionText,
            subject: `Experiment 12.1: Plastic Rock Simulation - ${extractedName}`
        };
        
        showStatus('Submitting results...', '');
        
        // Send email using EmailJS
        emailjs.send('service_ot1jg6s', 'template_y556rhr', templateParams)
            .then(function(response) {
                console.log('SUCCESS!', response.status, response.text);
                showStatus('Results submitted successfully!', 'success');
            }, function(error) {
                console.log('FAILED...', error);
                showStatus('Failed to submit results. Please try again.', 'error');
            });
    }
    
    function showStatus(message, type) {
        const statusElement = document.getElementById('submissionStatus');
        statusElement.textContent = message;
        statusElement.className = type;
    }
</script>

<script type="text/javascript" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@emailjs/browser@3/dist/email.min.js"></script>
<script type="text/javascript">
    (function() {
        emailjs.init("ct_s19oZHppLy9BlW");
    })();
</script>
